102. Why constructor injection is preferred over field injection?
-->Constructor injection is preferred because it makes dependencies mandatory, supports immutability, improves unit testing, and makes the code easier to maintain.

Example of constructor injection:
@Service
public class StudentService {

    private final StudentRepository studentRepository;

    public StudentService(StudentRepository studentRepository) {
        this.studentRepository = studentRepository;
    }
}


Example of field injection:
@Service
public class StudentService {

    @Autowired
    private StudentRepository studentRepository;
}


Advantages of constructor injection:
- Ensures required dependencies are provided when an object is created.
- Allows fields to be declared final.
- Makes unit testing easier without requiring Spring to inject dependencies.
- Helps detect missing dependencies during application startup.
Interview tip: Since Spring 4.3, if a class has only one constructor, @Autowired is not required.












103. If two beans of same type exist what happens?
-->If Spring finds two beans of the same type and cannot determine which one to inject, it throws a NoUniqueBeanDefinitionException.
        
        Example:
        public interface MessageService {
            void sendMessage();
        }
        
        
        @Service
        public class EmailService implements MessageService {
            public void sendMessage() {
                System.out.println("Email sent");
            }
        }
        
        
        @Service
        public class SmsService implements MessageService {
            public void sendMessage() {
                System.out.println("SMS sent");
            }
        }
        
        
        Now, injecting MessageService without specifying which implementation to use can cause ambiguity.
        
        Solution 1: Use @Primary
        @Service
        @Primary
        public class EmailService implements MessageService {
            public void sendMessage() {
                System.out.println("Email sent");
            }
        }
        
        
        Solution 2: Use @Qualifier
        @Service
        public class NotificationService {
        
            private final MessageService messageService;
        
            public NotificationService(
                    @Qualifier("smsService") MessageService messageService) {
                this.messageService = messageService;
            }
        }
        
        
        Other options include injecting all implementations using a List<MessageService> or Map<String, MessageService>.
        
        Interview tip: @Primary defines the default bean; @Qualifier selects a specific bean.
        











